{
	title: 'Shared continuous heat curve and web model quota fill',
	spec: "Terminal and web percentage-based heat indicators share the existing terminal continuous OKLCH curve: lightness 0.78, chroma 0.14, hue 145 minus 1.2 times percentage used clamped to 0..100. The web uses this curve wherever percentage heat is shown, including context usage in the compact status and details and subscription usage windows in details; it does not use three cool/warm/hot thresholds. Colours remain owned by the shared colour/theme machinery, honour host theme overrides, and meet the readable-text invariant without opacity fading. The web compact status model display name doubles as a quota-remaining indicator for the subscription account that the session next uses. It selects the shortest reported usage window by duration, normally 5h, not the most exhausted window. Its left portion spans the percentage remaining and has one solid colour from the shared curve at the percentage used; the right portion is readable neutral grey. Use CSS text clipping with a hard stop, so the boundary may bisect a glyph or pass invisibly through a space; do not split text into characters or use a rainbow gradient. At full remaining quota the name is fully green; as quota runs down the coloured width shrinks and the coloured portion moves through yellow and orange to red; at zero remaining it is fully grey. Without quota data, including API-key models, retain the ordinary neutral name rather than implying full or exhausted quota. An accessible description identifies the selected window and percentage remaining. Keep existing status-button interaction, truncation, modal details and stable DOM identity; update from pushed stats without browser polling. No new dependency. Test meaningful window selection and unavailable-data behaviour; check partial-glyph clipping in a real browser. On wide (desktop) layouts the compact status has room to spare, so it also shows the selected account's usage windows in full after the model name: per window (5h, 7d; model-specific windows such as 7d_sonnet are omitted, as in /status) a small bar coloured from the shared curve, the percentage used and the reset time in 24-hour local time (with a day when not today), from the same data shape as /status. It drops this detail progressively as width shrinks (reset times first, then the bars, then the windows) so it never wraps or pushes other status items off; phones keep only the coloured name. Clicking it opens the existing details. The wide layout also uses its room for identity: the first line shows the session id before the name, and the second line the full cwd, cut from the start when too long so its final directory stays visible; narrow layouts keep the name alone and the final cwd component. Where the windows show, the model name text stays neutral (the user's decision): the windows already show the quota, so the fill appears only once they are dropped.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Wide/narrow is a container query on .StatusRow (thresholds in ch), not a media query. In CDP tests, innerText of a display:none element returns its textContent, so check checkVisibility() first; wait two animation frames after setDeviceMetricsOverride before measuring. The user decided wide layouts keep the model name neutral, since the windows already show the quota.'
	],
	needs: ['1w', 'a0', '61']
}
